      "inlineEmojis": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1534571111357091971",
      "type": "Default",
      "timestamp": "2026-08-05T14:37:43.9+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": "2026-08-05T14:38:42.916+00:00",
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "If properly designed output drivers can indeed also be used as ESD protection. This is using the so-called snap-back behavior. I believe [this](https://siliconvlsi.com/explain-the-snapback-phenomenon-in-nmos-devices/) should explain something. I used 0.18um TSMC when @imec and both IO NMOS and PMOS were designed to have that capability.",
      "author": {
        "id": "728516105680846892",
        "name": "chips4makers",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "Chips4Makers aka Staf Verhaegen",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [],
        "avatarUrl": "2026-08_media/2649a6faac7843086b3d82b1d8f657a3-81998.png"
      },
      "attachments": [],
      "embeds": [
        {
          "title": "Explain the snapback phenomenon in NMOS devices - Siliconvlsi",
          "url": "https://siliconvlsi.com/explain-the-snapback-phenomenon-in-nmos-devices/",
          "timestamp": null,
          "description": "The snapback phenomenon in NMOS devices is an important aspect in understanding their behavior and reliability.",
          "thumbnail": {
            "url": "2026-08_media/Snapback-mechanism-in-NMOS-1-5DD4E.png",
            "width": 1366,
            "height": 768
          },
          "images": [],
          "fields": [],
